### New helpers

```go
// upstreamHasEntireSettings reports whether origin's default branch already
// contains .entire/settings.json. Returns (false, nil) when no resolvable
// remote-tracking ref exists — "no upstream we can see" is not an error.
func upstreamHasEntireSettings(ctx context.Context) (bool, error) {
    refs := []string{"origin/HEAD", "origin/main", "origin/master"}
    for _, ref := range refs {
        verify := exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", ref)
        if err := verify.Run(); err != nil {
            continue
        }
        cat := exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "cat-file", "-e", ref+":"+settings.EntireSettingsFile)
        if err := cat.Run(); err == nil {
            return true, nil
        }
        return false, nil
    }
    return false, nil
}

// applyCustomMetadataRef sets checkpoints_version: 1.1 in the project
// settings file and installs the fetch refspec on origin. Called only
// from runSetupFlow when a fresh repo was detected.
func applyCustomMetadataRef(ctx context.Context, w io.Writer) error {
    path, raw, _, err := settings.LoadProjectRaw(ctx)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("read project settings: %w", err)
    }
    var opts map[string]json.RawMessage
    if existing, ok := raw["strategy_options"]; ok {
        if err := json.Unmarshal(existing, &opts); err != nil {
            return fmt.Errorf("parse strategy_options: %w", err)
        }
    }
    if opts == nil {
        opts = map[string]json.RawMessage{}
    }
    opts["checkpoints_version"] = json.RawMessage("1.1")
    optsBytes, err := json.Marshal(opts)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    raw["strategy_options"] = optsBytes
    if err := settings.SaveProjectRaw(path, raw); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if err := installMetadataRefspec(ctx); err != nil {
        fmt.Fprintf(w, "Warning: failed to install fetch refspec for %s: %v\n", paths.MetadataRefName, err)
    }
    return nil
}

// installMetadataRefspec appends a fetch refspec on origin so subsequent
// `git fetch` calls pull refs/entire/checkpoints/v1 into the non-clobbering
// remote-tracking ref. Idempotent. No-op if origin doesn't exist or the
// refspec is already present.
func installMetadataRefspec(ctx context.Context) error {
    if err := exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "remote", "get-url", "origin").Run(); err != nil {
        return nil // no origin
    }
    refspec := "+" + paths.MetadataRefName + ":refs/entire/remotes/origin/checkpoints/v1"
    out, _ := exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "config", "--get-all", "remote.origin.fetch").Output()
    for _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), "\n") {
        if line == refspec {
            return nil
        }
    }
    return exec.CommandContext(ctx, "git", "config", "--add", "remote.origin.fetch", refspec).Run()
}
```

## Push hook refactor (`cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common.go`)

The current `pushBranchIfNeeded(ctx, target, branchName string)` hard-codes branch refs throughout (`plumbing.NewBranchReferenceName`, `refs/heads/...` in fetch refspecs, `refs/remotes/<remote>/...` for tracking). It is called from one production site: `manual_commit_push.go:36`. Its internal helpers (`hasUnpushedSessionsCommon`, `tryPushSessionsCommon`, `fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon`) are not called from anywhere else.

Refactor:

```go
// New signature, replaces pushBranchIfNeeded.
func pushRefIfNeeded(
    ctx context.Context,
    target string,
    localRef, trackingRef plumbing.ReferenceName,
) error
```

Internal helpers take ref-name strings instead of branch names. The fetch refspec built inside `fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon` is constructed from the passed-in refs rather than `refs/heads/<name>:refs/remotes/<remote>/<name>`. The rebase-on-non-FF flow (collect-local-only-commits → cherry-pick-onto-remote-tip → push merged result) is unchanged in behavior; only the ref names it operates on become parameterized.

Call site in `manual_commit_push.go`:

```go
err = pushRefIfNeeded(ctx, ps.pushTarget(),
    checkpoint.MetadataRef(ctx),
    checkpoint.MetadataTrackingRef(ctx))
```

Behavior:

- Legacy v1 repo: identical to today.
- 1.1 repo: same rebase-on-non-FF recovery flow at the custom ref pair.

This is the central reason 1.1 must not regress: it inherits today's mature branch-style rebase recovery rather than re-implementing it (which is what produced v2's push-conflict problems).

## Other commands and edge cases

### Commands that read the metadata ref

`entire doctor`, `entire resume`, `entire attach`, `entire clean`, `entire status`, `entire explain`, `entire search`, `entire trail` all read through `checkpoint/committed.go`, which uses the resolver after the call-site updates. No additional per-command changes beyond the inventory above.

### Shadow branches

Unchanged. Shadow branches (`entire/<commit[:7]>-<worktree[:6]>`) stay under `refs/heads/`. The orphan branch (today's metadata) is the only thing moving to a custom ref.

### Empty repo

`entire enable` on a repo with no commits works the same: settings.json is written; the metadata ref is created when the first checkpoint condenses. No special handling. Migration short-circuits (no legacy branch).

### No `origin` remote

`installMetadataRefspec` no-ops when `git remote get-url origin` fails. Upstream peek returns "no upstream". 1.1 is written. Pushes are no-ops (no target). Local-only operation works. Migration runs on first write if a legacy branch exists locally.

### Origin without resolvable default branch

Upstream peek tries `origin/HEAD`, then `origin/main`, then `origin/master`. If none resolve, treat as "no upstream", write 1.1.

### Upstream peek probe failure (unexpected git error)

Logged at Debug, treated as "no upstream", proceeds with 1.1 write. Failing open is preferred over blocking setup on a flake.

### Stale `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1` on a 1.1 repo after migration

By design. The legacy branch is the read-only artifact. Entire commands read only from the resolved ref; the stale branch is unreachable from v1.1 operations. Future `entire migrate cleanup-v1` can prune it.

### `checkpoints_version: 1.1` in `.entire/settings.local.json` only

The merged settings view returns 1.1 — local user's strategy uses the custom ref while the project says branch. Per-machine divergence. Not supported. Document: "Set `checkpoints_version` in `.entire/settings.json`, not in `settings.local.json`."

### Manual edit of project `settings.json` to add `checkpoints_version: 1.1` on an existing v1 repo

Strategy starts using the custom ref. The next checkpoint write triggers migration (mirror the legacy branch hash to the custom ref). The legacy branch is left untouched. Bob pulling Alice's settings.json change has the same migration trigger on Bob's first write. This is the **existing-repo opt-in path**.

### Legacy branch exists, custom ref exists, hashes diverge

Migration short-circuits (custom ref already populated). The custom ref is authoritative. The legacy branch sits stale at its older hash. No automatic reconciliation — the user would need to manually delete the custom ref to force re-migration. Documented as an unusual recovery scenario, not a supported operation.

## Error handling

| Failure | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Upstream peek probe fails | Logged at Debug, treated as no upstream, proceed with 1.1 write. |
| Upstream peek finds settings.json on origin | Hard error in `runSetupFlow`; nothing written locally. Message: "Existing Entire setup detected on origin/<branch>. Run `git pull` first to adopt it, then re-run `entire enable`." |
| `applyCustomMetadataRef` settings rewrite fails | Hard error from `runSetupFlow`. A half-applied 1.1 (no settings change) is preferable to a partially applied one. |
| `installMetadataRefspec` fails (config write error) | Warn to stderr; setup completes. The custom ref still works for the user that ran enable; the refspec just makes shared clones easier and is recoverable by re-running enable. |
| Migration `SetReference` fails (storer error) | Return the error from `EnsureMetadataBranch`; the surrounding write retries with normal error handling. Logged at WARN. The next write attempt retries migration. |
| Reading the legacy branch hash during migration fails | Log at WARN, fall through to fresh-orphan creation. The legacy branch is left untouched. |
| Push to custom ref hits non-FF | Existing branch-path recovery (`fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon`) executes against the custom ref pair via the refactored `pushRefIfNeeded`. Same machinery, same outcome as today's branch path. |
| Push to custom ref hits protected-ref rejection | Existing `printProtectedRefBlock` flow, same as branches. |

